          "citation": "489 U.S. 189 (1989)",
          "court": "Supreme Court of the United States",
          "year": 1989,
          "date_decided": "1989-02-22",
          "holding": "The Court held that social workers who received and recorded repeated abuse reports but did not remove the child owed him no affirmative substantive-due-process duty of protection, because the harm was inflicted by a private actor and the State had not taken him into custody.",
          "relevance": "Sets the constitutional consequence of a screen-out or unfounded closure: a later injury by the parent generally will not support a federal due-process claim against the agency.",
          "quote": "a State's failure to protect an individual against private violence simply does not constitute a violation of the Due Process Clause",
          "url": "https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/112202/deshaney-v-winnebago-cty-dss/",
